= Introduction

Conflict-free Replicated Data-Types (CRDTs) allow for simultaneous changes on shared state in a distributed system.
The properties of such CRDTs have been examined and verified in several papers.
We seek to find similarities, differences, as well as possible gaps in the verification tooling, methods, and process.

One of the core traits of CRTDs is the ability to merge two differing nodes, and create a new shared one. As such the mathematical requirements of merging are required. These include commutativity, association and idempotence. A verification tool therefore ideally needs to check for all of these properties.

When a conflict between updates to a CRDT arise, those can present themselves in many possible orders. 
The more complex the semantics of the data type used by the CRDT, the more different interleavings are possible. 
This set of possible interleavings grows exponentially, making an automatic verification highly beneficial @zhang_model-checking-driven_2024.


= Literature overview
One key problem described in several scientific contributions is the occurence of differing data states in CRTD implementations that occur when merging #cite(<zhu_crust_2025>) #cite(<zhang_model-checking-driven_2024>).
#cite(<zhang_model-checking-driven_2024>, form: "prose") describe the implementation of CRDTS as "notoriously difficult to [do] correctly".
As one potential reason for this difficulty they introduce the concept of _deep bugs_: Errors in the design or implementation of CRDTs that due to the exponential nature of the problem space are hard to replicate, but can manifest themselves in critical situations - mainly in large scale deployments @zhang_model-checking-driven_2024.

