= Verification and testing

#cite(<zhang_model-checking-driven_2024>, form: "author") suggest a testing explorative model-checking-driven testing framework (MET).
The formal model-checking is conducted using the formal specification language TLA+ #footnote[#link("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TLA%2B")].
The test cases are then automatically generated from the traces generated by the model-checking tool.
The authors highlight the importance of automatic verification in the face of the exponential nature of the problem space. 
This implies the need for any automatic verification tool to understand the model designed for the CRDT (i.e. the model needs to be described in TLA+). 
Additionally MET can test implementations of the model for coding-bugs benefiting of the cost effectiveness of traditional testing.
This works by using the traces generated by the model-checking tool as a test oracle. 
The trace consists of states connected by state transitions that can be system events (e.g. client requests) an environment events (e.g. out-of-order message delivery).
The implementation is expected to generate the same order of states generated by the oracle given the same events.

== Propel
Propel, which is embedded in Scala, has been developed for validating and checking CRTD properties. Similarly to Crust, they check for association, commutativity and idempotency in addition to selectivity. It does this by using type-checks. When a function is associative for instance, it can be derived from the type ot be associative and thus the properties of association apply. This is done in cohesion with a property derivation tree; this allows proof for the property itself.

This is based on an algorithm that causes rewrites and case analysis for any function to prove that e.g. commutativity holds. The rewrites open up the possibility of checking for equality (e.g. f(x, y) = f(y, x)). Case analysis is defined by them as pattern-matching for different cases to see if the algebraic rules hold up in each branch. 

This is done by introducing their own type system and compiler. Through that the properties are tested for at compile time #cite(<propel>).

In their paper, they bring up the example of an implementation of \#{ max } when merging two counters. The branch with the highest number should "win". Therefore equality of both branches need to be checked. We already know that we require commutativity, association and idempotence when merging. Equality now introduces the relational properties, in particular "reflexivity, symmetry and antisymmetry" #cite(<zakhour_type-checking_2023>). A regular implementation would not be commutative // better explanation to be found soon i guess. gotta re-read

Although this approach covers a large variety of proving the properties, it also fails to prove by counterexamples as of now.

